{"bendscript":"0.1","blocks":[{"id":"blk-76473","kind":"heading","level":1,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109683","text":"Skill 07 — Cognitive Science Foundations"}]},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76475","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109684","text":"Research grounding for each of the ten protocols: the theories, the papers, the design constraints, and the open questions. Eight cognitive primitives grounded in cognitive science / neuroscience, plus two cross-cutting protocols (OS-009 PRISM, OS-010 PULSE) grounded in psychometrics and closed-loop control theory."}]}],"id":"blk-76474","kind":"quote"},{"id":"blk-76476","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109685","text":"Why This Matters"}]},{"id":"blk-76477","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109686","text":"OpenSentience protocols are not arbitrary architecture decisions. Each one maps to a specific cognitive science finding about how intelligent systems manage knowledge, attention, and self-regulation. Understanding the research helps you understand "},{"id":"spn-109687","marks":["italic"],"text":"why"},{"id":"spn-109688","text":" the protocols work the way they do and where the boundaries of the analogy lie."}]},{"id":"blk-76478","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109689","text":"OS-001: Hippocampal Consolidation"}]},{"id":"blk-76479","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109690","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-109691","text":" McClelland, McNaughton & O'Reilly (1995). \"Why there are complementary learning systems in the hippocampus and neocortex.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76480","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109692","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109693","text":" The brain uses two complementary systems for memory. The hippocampus rapidly encodes new experiences (fast write). The neocortex slowly integrates this knowledge into long-term structure (consolidation). Replaying memories during sleep transfers hippocampal traces into neocortical representations without catastrophic forgetting."}]},{"id":"blk-76481","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109694","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-001:"}]},{"id":"blk-76482","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76484","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109695","text":"New knowledge must be stored immediately without disrupting existing memory"}]}],"id":"blk-76483","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76486","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109696","text":"Background consolidation must merge, decay, and prune to maintain quality"}]}],"id":"blk-76485","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76488","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109697","text":"Episodic memories (events) are stored differently from semantic memories (facts)"}]}],"id":"blk-76487","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76490","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109698","text":"Confidence decays over time unless reinforced by retrieval or outcomes"}]}],"id":"blk-76489","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76491","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109699","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-76492","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76494","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109700","text":"Immediate "},{"id":"spn-109701","marks":["code"],"text":"store_node"},{"id":"spn-109702","text":" writes (hippocampal fast-write analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-76493","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76496","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109703","marks":["code"],"text":"run_consolidation"},{"id":"spn-109704","text":" background cycle (neocortical integration analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-76495","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76498","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109705","text":"Three node types mapping to memory systems: episodic, semantic, procedural"}]}],"id":"blk-76497","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76499","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109706","text":"OS-002: Working Memory Gating"}]},{"id":"blk-76500","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109707","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-109708","text":" O'Reilly & Frank (2006). \"Making working memory work: A computational model of learning in the prefrontal cortex and basal ganglia.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76501","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109709","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109710","text":" The prefrontal cortex maintains active representations in working memory, but not everything gets in. The basal ganglia acts as a gate — deciding what information is relevant enough to enter working memory based on learned relevance signals and conflict detection."}]},{"id":"blk-76502","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109711","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-002:"}]},{"id":"blk-76503","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76505","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109712","text":"Not all retrieved knowledge should be acted upon equally"}]}],"id":"blk-76504","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76507","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109713","text":"Cyclic knowledge (contradictions, self-reinforcing claims) needs detection"}]}],"id":"blk-76506","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76509","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109714","text":"The kappa parameter serves as the conflict signal: kappa = 0 means no"}]}],"id":"blk-76508","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76510","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109715","text":"conflict (open the gate), kappa > 0 means conflict (engage deliberation)"}]},{"id":"blk-76511","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76513","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109716","text":"Routing must be fast for the common case (acyclic) and thorough for the"}]}],"id":"blk-76512","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76514","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109717","text":"rare case (cyclic)"}]},{"id":"blk-76515","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109718","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-76516","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76518","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109719","text":"SCC detection on retrieved neighborhoods (basal ganglia conflict signal)"}]}],"id":"blk-76517","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76520","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109720","text":"Kappa computation (gating threshold)"}]}],"id":"blk-76519","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76522","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109721","text":"Fast vs deliberate routing based on kappa value"}]}],"id":"blk-76521","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76523","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109722","text":"OS-003: Dual-Process Theory"}]},{"id":"blk-76524","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109723","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-109724","text":" Kahneman (2011). \"Thinking, Fast and Slow.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76525","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109725","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109726","text":" Human cognition operates in two modes. System 1 is fast, automatic, and heuristic-based — it handles routine decisions effortlessly. System 2 is slow, effortful, and analytical — it engages when System 1 encounters novelty, conflict, or high stakes."}]},{"id":"blk-76526","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109727","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-003:"}]},{"id":"blk-76527","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76529","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109728","text":"Most agent decisions should be fast (System 1 analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-76528","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76531","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109729","text":"Deliberation should engage only when triggered by conflict signals (kappa > 0)"}]}],"id":"blk-76530","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76533","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109730","text":"The deliberation protocol must structure argumentation (bid, debate, vote,"}]}],"id":"blk-76532","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76534","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109731","text":"commit) to resolve conflict rather than amplify it"}]},{"id":"blk-76535","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76537","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109732","text":"Timeout mechanisms prevent deliberation from stalling indefinitely"}]}],"id":"blk-76536","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76538","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109733","marks":["bold"],"text":"What AgenTroMatic implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-76539","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76541","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109734","text":"Four-phase deliberation pipeline (bid/debate/vote/commit)"}]}],"id":"blk-76540","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76543","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109735","text":"Argumentation framework with claims, warrants, and rebuttals"}]}],"id":"blk-76542","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76545","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109736","text":"Consensus and escalation mechanisms"}]}],"id":"blk-76544","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76546","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109737","text":"OS-004: Endogenous Attention"}]},{"id":"blk-76547","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109738","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-109739","text":" Desimone & Duncan (1995). \"Neural mechanisms of selective visual attention.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76548","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109740","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109741","text":" Attention is not just stimulus-driven (exogenous — a loud noise grabs your attention). It is also goal-directed (endogenous — you look for your car keys because you intend to drive). Endogenous attention biases perception toward goal-relevant information, filtering out distractions."}]},{"id":"blk-76549","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109742","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-004:"}]},{"id":"blk-76550","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76552","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109743","text":"The attention engine must prioritize based on active goals, not just recency"}]}],"id":"blk-76551","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76553","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109744","text":"or novelty"}]},{"id":"blk-76554","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76556","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109745","text":"Salience scoring combines urgency, recency, goal relevance, and confidence"}]}],"id":"blk-76555","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76558","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109746","text":"The survey/triage/dispatch cycle mirrors the scan/filter/act structure of"}]}],"id":"blk-76557","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76559","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109747","text":"biological attention"}]},{"id":"blk-76560","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76562","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109748","text":"Dispatch modes (act, learn, escalate, idle) prevent attention from always"}]}],"id":"blk-76561","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76563","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109749","text":"demanding action"}]},{"id":"blk-76564","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109750","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-76565","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76567","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109751","marks":["code"],"text":"attention_survey"},{"id":"spn-109752","text":" (endogenous scan of goal-relevant state)"}]}],"id":"blk-76566","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76569","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109753","marks":["code"],"text":"attention_run_cycle"},{"id":"spn-109754","text":" (full survey/triage/dispatch loop)"}]}],"id":"blk-76568","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76571","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109755","text":"Goal bias in salience scoring"}]}],"id":"blk-76570","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76572","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109756","text":"OS-005: Resource Rationality"}]},{"id":"blk-76573","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109757","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-109758","text":" Lieder & Griffiths (2020). \"Resource-rational analysis: Understanding human cognition as the optimal use of limited computational resources.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76574","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109759","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109760","text":" Optimal cognition is not about always computing the best answer. It is about allocating computational effort proportional to the value of the decision. Simple decisions warrant fast, cheap heuristics. High-stakes decisions justify expensive, thorough analysis."}]},{"id":"blk-76575","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109761","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-005:"}]},{"id":"blk-76576","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76578","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109762","text":"Three model tiers map to three levels of computational investment"}]}],"id":"blk-76577","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76580","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109763","text":"The same tool surface and topology are available at every tier"}]}],"id":"blk-76579","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76582","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109764","text":"Escalation rules define when to upgrade from cheap to expensive processing"}]}],"id":"blk-76581","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76584","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109765","text":"Budget constraints (tokens, latency, cost) prevent unbounded computation"}]}],"id":"blk-76583","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76585","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109766","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous and Agentelic implement:"}]},{"id":"blk-76586","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76588","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109767","text":"local_small / local_large / cloud_frontier tier definitions"}]}],"id":"blk-76587","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76590","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109768","text":"Escalation thresholds based on confidence and decision stakes"}]}],"id":"blk-76589","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76592","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109769","text":"Token and latency budgets per tier"}]}],"id":"blk-76591","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76593","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109770","text":"OS-006: Executive Function"}]},{"id":"blk-76594","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109771","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-109772","text":" Miyake, Friedman et al. (2000). \"The unity and diversity of executive functions and their contributions to complex frontal lobe tasks.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76595","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109773","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109774","text":" Executive function is an umbrella term for the cognitive processes that regulate, control, and manage other cognitive processes. Miyake et al. identified three core components: inhibitory control (suppressing inappropriate responses), task switching (flexibly shifting between tasks), and working memory updating (monitoring and revising held information)."}]},{"id":"blk-76596","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109775","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-006:"}]},{"id":"blk-76597","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76599","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109776","text":"Inhibitory control maps to the permission system (blocking disallowed actions)"}]}],"id":"blk-76598","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76601","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109777","text":"Task switching maps to the lifecycle state machine (managing agent state)"}]}],"id":"blk-76600","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76603","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109778","text":"Working memory updating maps to graduated autonomy (adapting the level of"}]}],"id":"blk-76602","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76604","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109779","text":"agent independence based on accumulated trust)"}]},{"id":"blk-76605","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76607","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109780","text":"The audit trail provides metacognitive monitoring — awareness of what the"}]}],"id":"blk-76606","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76608","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109781","text":"system has done"}]},{"id":"blk-76609","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109782","marks":["bold"],"text":"What `open_sentience` implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-76610","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76612","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109783","text":"PermissionEngine (inhibitory control)"}]}],"id":"blk-76611","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76614","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109784","text":"AgentLifecycle GenStateMachine (task switching)"}]}],"id":"blk-76613","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76616","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109785","text":"AutonomyController (working memory updating / cognitive flexibility)"}]}],"id":"blk-76615","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76618","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109786","text":"AuditWriter (metacognitive monitoring)"}]}],"id":"blk-76617","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76619","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109787","text":"OS-007: Adversarial Robustness — Self/Non-Self Discrimination"}]},{"id":"blk-76620","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109788","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theoretical grounding:"},{"id":"spn-109789","text":" Adaptive immunity theory (Burnet 1959; Janeway 1989)."}]},{"id":"blk-76621","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109790","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109791","text":" Biological immune systems distinguish self from non-self through a combination of innate pattern recognition and adaptive memory. The same architecture maps cleanly to agent threat detection: known-good behavior is \"self,\" novel attack patterns are \"non-self,\" and the system must learn to recognize new threats without misclassifying legitimate variation."}]},{"id":"blk-76622","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109792","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-007:"}]},{"id":"blk-76623","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76625","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109793","text":"Five threat categories with explicit detection rules"}]}],"id":"blk-76624","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76627","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109794","text":"Defense protocols must be reversible (quarantine before destroy)"}]}],"id":"blk-76626","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76629","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109795","text":"Integration with OS-006 for permission revocation and OS-008 for circuit"}]}],"id":"blk-76628","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76630","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109796","text":"breaking — the immune response is enacted by the governance and harness layers"}]},{"id":"blk-76631","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109797","text":"OS-008: Supervisory Attentional System"}]},{"id":"blk-76632","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109798","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-109799","text":" Norman & Shallice (1986). \"Attention to action: Willed and automatic control of behavior.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76633","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109800","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109801","text":" Routine behavior runs automatically through contention scheduling between learned action schemas. A "},{"id":"spn-109802","marks":["italic"],"text":"supervisory attentional system"},{"id":"spn-109803","text":" intervenes when novel, dangerous, or constraint-violating situations arise. The supervisory system does not execute behavior directly — it modulates which schemas are allowed to run."}]},{"id":"blk-76634","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109804","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-008:"}]},{"id":"blk-76635","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76637","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109805","text":"The harness is the runtime that calls the agent, not a tool the agent calls"}]}],"id":"blk-76636","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76639","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109806","text":"It enforces pipeline ordering, quality gates, sprint contracts, and context"}]}],"id":"blk-76638","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76640","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109807","text":"management"}]},{"id":"blk-76641","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76643","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109808","text":"It intervenes when prerequisites are not met or when quality thresholds are"}]}],"id":"blk-76642","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76644","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109809","text":"not crossed — but it does not generate the agent's outputs"}]},{"id":"blk-76645","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109810","text":"OS-009: PRISM — Meta-Cognition + Psychometrics"}]},{"id":"blk-76646","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109811","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key references:"}]},{"id":"blk-76647","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76649","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109812","text":"Rasch, G. (1960). \"Probabilistic models for some intelligence and attainment"}]}],"id":"blk-76648","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76650","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109813","text":"tests.\" (Item Response Theory foundation)"}]},{"id":"blk-76651","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76653","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109814","text":"Lord, F. M. (1980). \"Applications of item response theory to practical testing"}]}],"id":"blk-76652","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76654","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109815","text":"problems.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76655","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76657","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109816","text":"Green, D. M. & Swets, J. A. (1966). \"Signal detection theory and"}]}],"id":"blk-76656","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76658","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109817","text":"psychophysics.\""}]},{"id":"blk-76659","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76661","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109818","text":"Flavell, J. H. (1979). \"Metacognition and cognitive monitoring.\" (Meta-cognition)"}]}],"id":"blk-76660","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76662","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109819","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109820","text":" Measuring whether a system "},{"id":"spn-109821","marks":["italic"],"text":"learns"},{"id":"spn-109822","text":" (rather than merely "},{"id":"spn-109823","marks":["italic"],"text":"answers"},{"id":"spn-109824","text":") requires the same toolkit psychometricians built for measuring human learning: calibrated item difficulty, separation of item-quality from learner-quality parameters, and detection of response bias. PRISM applies IRT to scenario calibration and signal detection theory to dimension scoring, then closes the meta-cognitive loop by reflecting on its own scenarios and evolving them."}]},{"id":"blk-76663","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109825","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-009:"}]},{"id":"blk-76664","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76666","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109826","text":"9 continual-learning dimensions (retention, plasticity, transfer,"}]}],"id":"blk-76665","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76667","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109827","text":"contradiction handling, etc.) — each with calibrated scoring rubrics"}]},{"id":"blk-76668","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76670","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109828","text":"4-phase evaluation loop: compose → interact → observe → reflect → diagnose"}]}],"id":"blk-76669","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76671","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109829","text":"(the diagnostic must itself be a closed loop)"}]},{"id":"blk-76672","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76674","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109830","text":"BYOR ingestion — point PRISM at any repo and it generates scenarios"}]}],"id":"blk-76673","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76676","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109831","text":"IRT calibration of scenario difficulty across cycles"}]}],"id":"blk-76675","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76678","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109832","marks":["bold"],"text":"PULSE-aware:"},{"id":"spn-109833","text":" PRISM's "},{"id":"spn-109834","marks":["code"],"text":"interact"},{"id":"spn-109835","text":" phase reads any system's PULSE manifest"}]}],"id":"blk-76677","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76679","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109836","text":"at runtime and drives the inner loop through its declared phases"}]},{"id":"blk-76680","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109837","text":"OS-010: PULSE — Closed-Loop Control Theory + Temporal Cognition"}]},{"id":"blk-76681","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109838","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key references:"}]},{"id":"blk-76682","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76684","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109839","text":"Wiener, N. (1948). \"Cybernetics: or Control and Communication in the Animal"}]}],"id":"blk-76683","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76685","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109840","text":"and the Machine.\" (Closed-loop control foundation)"}]},{"id":"blk-76686","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76688","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109841","text":"Allen, J. F. (1983). \"Maintaining knowledge about temporal intervals.\""}]}],"id":"blk-76687","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76689","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109842","text":"(Interval algebra)"}]},{"id":"blk-76690","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76692","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109843","text":"CloudEvents v1 specification (CNCF, 2019). (Event envelope standard)"}]}],"id":"blk-76691","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76693","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109844","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-109845","text":" Wiener's cybernetics established that intelligent behavior — in animals or machines — requires closed feedback loops with explicit phases: sense, decide, act, observe, adjust. Allen's interval algebra formalized the 13 possible relationships between temporal intervals, providing a vocabulary for describing how loops can nest, overlap, and signal one another. PULSE combines these into a manifest standard: every loop in the [&] portfolio declares its phases in the same vocabulary, and cross-loop signals use CloudEvents v1 envelopes so that loops can compose without bespoke adapters."}]},{"id":"blk-76694","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109846","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-010:"}]},{"id":"blk-76695","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76697","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109847","text":"5 canonical phase kinds ("},{"id":"spn-109848","marks":["code"],"text":"retrieve"},{"id":"spn-109849","text":", "},{"id":"spn-109850","marks":["code"],"text":"route"},{"id":"spn-109851","text":", "},{"id":"spn-109852","marks":["code"],"text":"act"},{"id":"spn-109853","text":", "},{"id":"spn-109854","marks":["code"],"text":"learn"},{"id":"spn-109855","text":", "},{"id":"spn-109856","marks":["code"],"text":"consolidate"},{"id":"spn-109857","text":")"}]}],"id":"blk-76696","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76698","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109858","text":"cover the closed-loop control archetype; custom phases extend it without breaking the schema"}]},{"id":"blk-76699","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76701","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109859","text":"5 canonical cross-loop tokens ("},{"id":"spn-109860","marks":["code"],"text":"TopologyContext"},{"id":"spn-109861","text":", "},{"id":"spn-109862","marks":["code"],"text":"DeliberationResult"},{"id":"spn-109863","text":","}]}],"id":"blk-76700","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76702","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109864","marks":["code"],"text":"OutcomeSignal"},{"id":"spn-109865","text":", "},{"id":"spn-109866","marks":["code"],"text":"ReputationUpdate"},{"id":"spn-109867","text":", "},{"id":"spn-109868","marks":["code"],"text":"ConsolidationEvent"},{"id":"spn-109869","text":") cover the observed inter-system signaling needs"}]},{"id":"blk-76703","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76705","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109870","text":"7 invariants (phase atomicity, feedback immutability, append-only audit,"}]}],"id":"blk-76704","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76706","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109871","text":"kappa routing, quorum before commit, outcome grounding, trace ID propagation) encode the structural correctness conditions"}]},{"id":"blk-76707","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-76709","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-109872","text":"A 12-test conformance suite makes \"PULSE-conforming\" objectively verifiable"}]}],"id":"blk-76708","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-76710","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-109873","text":"Open Research Questions"}]}